\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For $V\in C^{2}(R^d)$ with $V(0)=0$, $\nabla V(0)=0$ and $I\leq \nabla ^{2}V(x)\leq 2I$ everywhere, can its Gibbs law be sampled to total-variation error $\leq 1/10$ using at most $C_\varepsilon d^\varepsilon $ exact $(V,\nabla V)$ oracle queries on every execution, for each fixed $\varepsilon >0$, with unrestricted real computation between queries? Is the optimal query budget also at least c log d?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For $V\in C^{2}(R^d)$ with $V(0)=0$, $\nabla V(0)=0$ and $I\leq \nabla ^{2}V(x)\leq 2I$ everywhere, can its Gibbs law be sampled to total-variation error $\leq 1/10$ using at most $C_\varepsilon d^\varepsilon $ exact $(V,\nabla V)$ oracle queries on every execution, for each fixed $\varepsilon >0$, with unrestricted real computation between queries? Is the optimal query budget also at least c log d?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2021/59}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Subpolynomial-query-complexity-for-well-conditioned-log-concave-sampling-September-26-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

