\section*{Zero dimension exponent for well-conditioned sampling queries}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 139; 2026-09-26), the asserted result is that exact value-and-gradient sampling for known-minimizer potentials with Hessian between $I$ and $2I$ has worst-case query budget at most $C_\varepsilon d^\varepsilon$, with a logarithmic lower bound and unrestricted internal computation.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 139; 2026-09-26), transport-derived skew dynamics keep a stationary Gaussian-observation flow whose momentum records a conditional mean. Material-derivative bounds and nested noisy-center evaluations control the query count; stability and deterministic caps convert the analytic construction to a sampling procedure.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 139; 2026-09-26).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 139; 2026-09-26), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: exact value-and-gradient sampling for known-minimizer potentials with Hessian between $I$ and $2I$ has worst-case query budget at most $C_\varepsilon d^\varepsilon$, with a logarithmic lower bound and unrestricted internal computation.

The selected statement includes the clause used for this problem.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{LogConcaveQuery.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.LogConcaveSampling.exact\_source\_main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

